  "slug": "cost-compression-469c",
  "summary": "Three new preprints give certification criteria for nonstabilizerness, alongside tighter capacity, entropy and recovery-map bounds for qubit channels and states.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the operational characterization of nonstabilizerness (magic) in multi-qubit systems. One preprint certifies magic from few copies using moments of the discrete Wigner function. Another uses a stabilizer pigeonhole criterion with worst-case random access codes, covering states including W states. A third proves that multi-qubit bound magic exists, meaning states that cannot be distilled to pure magic, via a PPT-like criterion closed under tensor products. A second thread tightens quantitative bounds in quantum Shannon theory. These include a two-way-assisted capacity upper bound for amplitude-damping channels that is strictly tighter than PLOB, and sharp purity-based continuity bounds for von Neumann entropy with machine-learning-assisted estimation. A semidefinite-programming framework unifies pretty good measurements and Petz recovery maps. Separately, the classical complexity of state transmission is identified with the classical communication needed to simulate quantum correlations.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-26"
}
